.TH WC 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
wc \- count lines, words, and bytes (or characters) in files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wc
.RB [ \-c | \-m ]
.RB [ \-l ]
.RB [ \-w ]
.RI [ file ]\ .\|.\|.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wc
reads each
.I file
and, by default, writes the number of newline characters, words, and
bytes it contains. When one or more of
.BR \-c ", " \-l ", " \-m ", "
or
.B \-w
is given,
.B wc
reports only the counts requested \(em but always in the fixed
order newlines, words, then bytes\-or\-characters, regardless of the
order the options were given on the command line.
.PP
Each output line has the form
.RS
.nf
\fInewlines words bytes\fR \fIfile\fR
.fi
.RE
with any of the three counts omitted according to the options given.
No
.I file
field is printed when a count is being taken from standard input with
no operands. When more than one
.I file
operand is given, a final line labeled
.B total
is printed with the sums across every file that was read
successfully.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-c
Report the byte count. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-m ;
giving both is a usage error.
.TP
.B \-l
Report the newline count.
.TP
.B \-m
Report the character count instead of the byte count. Input is
decoded as UTF\-8: a byte sequence that cannot be decoded counts as
one character and is skipped one byte at a time, and a multibyte
sequence still incomplete at end\-of\-file is likewise counted one
byte at a time, so no trailing bytes are ever silently dropped from
the count. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-c .
.TP
.B \-w
Report the word count. A word is a maximal non\-empty run of
characters delimited by whitespace, classified byte\-by\-byte; this
is exact for UTF\-8 input, since no continuation or lead byte of a
multibyte sequence is ever itself classified as whitespace.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I file
A pathname of an input file. If
.B \-
is given, standard input is used at that point. If no
.I file
operand is given at all, standard input is used and no filename is
printed.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All input files were counted successfully.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred (an invalid option,
.B \-c
and
.B \-m
given together, a count that overflowed, or a file that could not be
opened or read); processing continues with the next operand, and a
file that failed is excluded from the
.B total
line.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR head (1),
.BR tail (1),
.BR cut (1)
